<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Hamilton-Gordon, 1st Baron Stanmore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Arthur Gordon, Arthur Gordon, Lord Stanmore, Arthur Hamilton-Gordon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Hamilton-Gordon, 1st Baron Stanmore, was a British colonial governor and the youngest son of the prime minister Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>. As the first substantive governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> from 1875 to 1880 he ruled through the chiefs and a Great Council of Chiefs, protected Fijian land from sale and brought indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to work the plantations. He also governed New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-hamilton-gordon-1st-baron-stanmore.jpg" alt="Arthur Hamilton-Gordon, 1st Baron Stanmore" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Arthur_Hamilton_Gordon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Charles Hamilton-Gordon was born in 1829 at Argyll House, his family's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> townhouse, the youngest son of George Hamilton-Gordon, 4th Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, and his second wife, Harriet Douglas, widow of Viscount Hamilton. Educated privately and at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, he was President of the Cambridge Union Society in 1849. After graduating in 1851 he served as assistant private secretary to his father as prime minister from 1852 to 1855, and sat as Liberal Member of Parliament for Beverley from 1854 to 1857.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As Lieutenant-Governor of New Brunswick from 1861 to 1866 he secured the province's assent to Canadian Confederation. He went on to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> from 1866 to 1870 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> from 21 February 1871 to 18 August 1874. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> he was also Consul-General and, from 1877 to 1880, High Commissioner for the Western Pacific, posts that added little to his power. He then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 29 November 1880 to 24 June 1882 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> from 1883 to 1890. He was made a Knight Commander of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1871 and a Knight Grand Cross in 1878.</p><p>The ethnographic collection he had gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1878. Throughout his career he kept up a long correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a>, later published as the Gladstone-Gordon correspondence, 1851-1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was created Baron Stanmore, of Great Stanmore in the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a>, on 21 August 1893. In 1897 he became chairman of the Pacific Islands Company, founded by John T. Arundel, which mined phosphate rock on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a>, then called Ocean Island, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nauru">Nauru</a>; he and Arundel raised the money and negotiated with the German company that held the Nauru licences, and in 1902 the business merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaluit-gesellschaft">Jaluit Gesellschaft</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> to form the Pacific Phosphate Company. He joined the Royal Commission on Historical Manuscripts in 1900. He had married Rachel Emily Shaw Lefevre on 20 September 1865, and they had a daughter and a son. He died in Chelsea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 30 January 1912.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Hamilton-Gordon, 1st Baron Stanmore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first governor, <b>Sir Arthur Gordon</b>, laid down the colony's lasting system, and the capital moved from Levuka to Suva in 1882.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-fiji">Colony of Fiji</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1882" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arthur Hamilton-Gordon, 1st Baron Stanmore"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1882">What was here in 1882? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
